    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Much of the methodology of quantitative genetics relies on the comparison of phenotypic measures in close relatives. A common application involves the regression of offspring on parent, which was one of Wright's (1921a) earliest uses of path analysis. Defining an individual's phenotype (z) to be the sum of its genotypic value (G) and an environmental deviation (E), the phenotypes of a father (f) and mother (m) may be written",
