    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Quantitative geneticists have long referred to the fraction of phenotypic variance that is additive genetic in basis as the narrow-sense heritability and abbreviated it as $ h^{2} $. The use of this notation, particularly the square, may seem puzzling. Returning to Figure A2.3, the origin of $ h^{2} $ can now be seen to be a historical tribute to Wright's (1921a) path diagram. Under the additive model of gene action, the equation of complete determination for an individual's phenotype is",
      "source_page": 841,
      "printed_page": 821,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p023:b9"
      ],
      "bbox": [
